{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c66d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae11-04bd-7bb0-aac5-3ecca198188f"},"sequence":401,"recorded_at":1780531400000797,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"a44b1373-1c97-4578-936f-97327477c22e","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"a44b1373-1c97-4578-936f-97327477c22e","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"a44b1373-1c97-4578-936f-97327477c22e"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae11-f674-7f92-91f9-4201a0f11025"},"task_id":"01a0ae11-f674-7f92-91f9-4201a0f11025","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ae11-f674-7f92-91f9-4201a0f11025","chunk":"Read text file `internal/provhost/profile_resolve.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/secprim\"\n5|)\n6|\n7|// This file resolves Section 7.7 execution-profile mappings against the\n8|// exact probed provider build and projects the sanitized launch argv a\n9|// launch or resume operation executes. The table in profile.go is the\n10|// single source of the required yolo flags; this file decides when\n11|// those flags may be applied.\n12|//\n13|// Resolution requires the exact-version probe result as a BuildTuple:\n14|// the tuple passes the Section 8.4 resume gate (CheckResumeTuple, so\n15|// an unknown provider, platform, or architecture, a direct Qwen\n16|// claim, and an unverified Muse build refuse before any mapping is\n17|// considered) and names the resolved provider exactly, because a\n18|// mapping decided for one build must never silently apply to\n19|// another. A provider without a Section 7.7 row — Qwen direct,\n20|// future plugins — and a Pi build other than the table's pinned\n21|// 0.73.1 cannot be mapped for the probed version and refuse with\n22|// the Section 2.4 profile_mapping_unavailable resume failure.\n23|//\n24|// The launch projection emits the unrestricted flag only for yolo\n25|// and only the exact table token: an absent flag (nothing to emit\n26|// where yolo requires one is impossible by construction, so absence\n27|// is the caller's empty base, refused structurally) or a changed\n28|// flag (any table token already present in the base, or any\n29|// foreign table token) fails closed. Standard omits every\n30|// unrestricted flag and refuses a base that already carries one —\n31|// the decidable half of the machine-local-alias rule: an exact\n32|// token match over the six table flags plus the documented codex\n33|// --yolo alias. A locally aliased shell word that expands to\n34|// unrestricted mode without spelling a table token is beyond what\n35|// argv inspection can decide (shell aliases never expand under\n36|// execve argv, which is the structural defense, owned by secprim);\n37|// that residual is stated, not silently admitted.\n38|//\n39|// The Section 5.1 argv bounds enforced here (1..128 elements, each\n40|// 1..4,096 bytes, 65,536 bytes total) are the host-side twin of\n41|// the SpawnPlan wire bounds checkSpawnArgv enforces: the two never\n42|// diverge, pinned by the agreement test that drives identical\n43|// vectors at each limit through both entries. Structural argv\n44|// validity (non-empty, NUL-free, UTF-8) stays with secprim and is\n45|// driven, not duplicated.\n46|\n47|// codexAliasYOLO is the codex --yolo alias Section 7.7 documents as\n48|// accepted by the current CLI. The required adapter mapping stays\n49|// the long form in profileYOLOMapping; the alias exists here only\n50|// so the launch projection recognizes (and refuses) it as an\n51|// unrestricted token rather than passing it through as an\n52|// innocuous word.\n53|const codexAliasYOLO = \"--yolo\"\n54|\n55|// piPinnedVersion is the exact Pi build the Section 7.7 table maps.\n56|// Every other Pi version refuses profile_mapping_unavailable:\n57|// the table pins 0.73.1, and no other row covers Pi.\n58|const piPinnedVersion = \"0.73.1\"\n59|\n60|// ResolvedMapping is one Section 7.7 mapping decision for an exact\n61|// probed build: the provider and profile it was resolved for, the\n62|// probed version it is bound to, the SpawnPlan profile_mapping\n63|// value (the exact table flag for yolo, empty for standard, which\n64|// omits every unrestricted flag), and whether both profiles share\n65|// the provider's default full tool set (Pi only — probe and launch\n66|// output MUST disclose that equivalence).\n67|type ResolvedMapping struct {\n68|\tProviderID string\n69|\tProfile    string\n70|\tVersion    string\n71|\tMapping    string\n72|\tEquivalent bool\n73|}\n74|\n75|// ResolveMapping resolves the Section 7.7 adapter mapping for one\n76|// provider under one profile against the exact probed build tuple.\n77|// The tuple passes the Section 8.4 resume gate and must name the\n78|// resolved provider; the profile must be inside the vocabulary; the\n79|// provider must carry a Section 7.7 row and Pi must be the pinned\n80|// 0.73.1 build. Unmappable combinations refuse with the Section\n81|// 2.4 profile_mapping_unavailable failure naming the provider,\n82|// probed version, and profile; miscorrelated inputs refuse as\n83|// caller errors.\n84|func ResolveMapping(providerID, profile string, tuple BuildTuple) (ResolvedMapping, error) {\n85|\tempty := ResolvedMapping{}\n86|\t// The caller's provider argument is classified before the\n87|\t// tuple gate runs: garbage fails as a caller error, while a\n88|\t// well-formed provider without a Section 7.7 row (Qwen\n89|\t// direct, future plugins) is the mapping gap Section 2.4\n90|\t// names. Running the tuple gate first would misclassify\n91|\t// both as tuple refusals.\n92|\tif !validProviderID(providerID) {\n93|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping provider is not a provider id\")\n94|\t\tif err != nil {\n95|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n96|\t\t}\n97|\t\treturn empty, failure\n98|\t}\n99|\tif _, ok := profileYOLOMapping[providerID]; !ok {\n100|\t\tfailure, err := failMappingUnavailable(\"mapping has no row for this provider\", providerID, tuple.ProviderVersion, profile)\n101|\t\tif err != nil {\n102|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n103|\t\t}\n104|\t\treturn empty, failure\n105|\t}\n106|\tif err := CheckResumeTuple(tuple); err != nil {\n107|\t\treturn empty, err\n108|\t}\n109|\tif tuple.ProviderID != providerID {\n110|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping tuple names another provider\")\n111|\t\tif err != nil {\n112|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n113|\t\t}\n114|\t\treturn empty, failure\n115|\t}\n116|\tif !profileNames[profile] {\n117|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping profile is not standard or yolo\")\n118|\t\tif err != nil {\n119|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n120|\t\t}\n121|\t\treturn empty, failure\n122|\t}\n123|\tif providerID == \"pi\" && tuple.ProviderVersion != piPinnedVersion {\n124|\t\tfailure, err := failMappingUnavailable(\"mapping pins pi to probed 0.73.1\", providerID, tuple.ProviderVersion, profile)\n125|\t\tif err != nil {\n126|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n127|\t\t}\n128|\t\treturn empty, failure\n129|\t}\n130|\t// The flag value comes from the single table source. Both\n131|\t// refusals below it are unreachable on this path — the row and\n132|\t// the profile were just established — but the value must not be\n133|\t// retyped here.\n134|\tmapping, err := ProfileMapping(providerID, profile)\n135|\tif err != nil {\n136|\t\treturn empty, err\n137|\t}\n138|\treturn ResolvedMapping{\n139|\t\tProviderID: providerID,\n140|\t\tProfile:    profile,\n141|\t\tVersion:    tuple.ProviderVersion,\n142|\t\tMapping:    mapping,\n143|\t\tEquivalent: providerID == \"pi\",\n144|\t}, nil\n145|}\n146|\n147|// ProjectLaunchArgv projects the sanitized launch argv for one\n148|// resolved mapping over the caller base (the provider binary with\n149|// its subcommand). The resolution must name this provider and\n150|// profile, and the base must be structurally valid. Yolo appends\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
